  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1228 PM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nSubtle and slow changes to the monsoon high begins to take hold \nSunday and through next week. H5 heights begin to lower to 595-596dm \nSunday and Monday while the center of the high remains over the Four \nCorners region to along the NM/CO border. This will allow high \ntemperatures to begin retreating from near record levels across \nnorthern and central NM. Scattered afternoon thunderstorm activity \nwill continue to favor development over the high terrain with lower \ncoverage closer to the Four Corners region. Storms will be slow \nmovers tracking slowly clockwise around the center of the high each \nday. East canyon winds pushing into Santa Fe and Albuquerque each \nevening also looks likely into early next week. \n\nTuesday and beyond sees the monsoon high begin to expand in area \nwhile also lowering in height and meandering eastward toward TX. \nThis will allow for a tap of monsoonal moisture from the Gulf of CA \ninto the Desert Southwest resulting in an uptick in PWs to the 1.00\" \nto 1.20\" by the later half of next week. Coverage of afternoon \nthunderstorms trends upwards with storms becoming more rain \nefficient producers. This will increase the risk of flash flooding \nwhere locally heavy rainfall does occur, notably over recent burn \nscars.",
